### 3. OpenLayers layer plugin

**Sample layer configuration:**

```json
{
    "type": "arcgis-feature",
    "url": "https://services2.arcgis.com/pROHh69WvVijk4nR/ArcGIS/rest/services/GSE_DISTRIBUTORI_STANDARD/FeatureServer",
    "title": "GSE Distributori Standard",
    "strategy": "tile",
    "geometryType": "MultiPolygon",
    "maxRecordCount": 2000,
    "visibility": true
}
```

Will be registered as `arcgis-feature` layer type using `VectorSource` + `VectorLayer`.

- Bbox extent (in map CRS) will be reprojected to EPSG:4326 via `reprojectBbox` before querying.
- Response will be read with `dataProjection: 'EPSG:4326', featureProjection: <mapCRS>` - OL + proj4 will handle reprojection to the map's CRS.
- Will dispatch  features load to correctly drive the global loading spinner for concurrent tile/bbox requests.
- Update triggers: security change, strategy change, style change, min/max resolution change.

---

### 4. CesiumJS layer plugin

Will use `GeoJSONStyledFeatures` for rendering. Cesium operates natively in EPSG:4326, so no client-side reprojection will be needed.

- `TiledBillboardCollection` will be used for point/multipoint layers under `tile` strategy.
- For non-point geometry types, `getEffectiveStrategy` will transparently downgrade `tile` → `bbox` since `TiledBillboardCollection` only supports point rendering.
- `bbox` strategy will use `camera.moveEnd` with a 300ms debounce.

---

### 5. Identify (GetFeatureInfo)

Will query `{url}/{layerId}/query` with a click-derived bbox in EPSG:4326 and `f=geojson`. Features will be returned directly - no ESRI-to-GeoJSON conversion needed.

---

### 6. Style editor

- `arcgis-feature` will be added to `isVectorStylableLayer` - enabling the Style tab in layer settings.
- `capabilitiesRequest` will resolve immediately with `layer.geometryType` from catalog metadata (no server round-trip). Feature collection will be resolved from `layer.features`.

---

### 7. Legend

`arcgis-feature` will render `VectorLegend` generated from the GeoStyler style (same as WFS/vector layers). FeatureServer does not expose a `/legend` endpoint, so no ArcGIS-specific legend component will be needed.

---

### 8. Persistence

`saveLayer` will persist `strategy`, `geometryType`, and `maxRecordCount` so they survive map save/reload.

---

### Design decisions

> [!NOTE]
**Default strategy: `tile`**
Tile strategy will provide the best balance of performance and UX for typical FeatureServer datasets. It will load features incrementally per tile grid cell, avoid fetching the entire dataset at once, and provide natural spatial partitioning.<br>
**Custom projection / CRS support**
All ArcGIS requests will use `inSR=4326` / `outSR=4326`, with bbox reprojection handled client-side via proj4. Any custom CRS defined in MapStore's `projectionDefs` (registered with proj4 at startup) will be fully supported — the map extent will be reprojected from the custom CRS to EPSG:4326 before querying, and response features will be reprojected back into the map's CRS by OpenLayers. This removes any dependency on ArcGIS recognizing the projection WKID.<br>
**Mixed geometries within a single layer**
ArcGIS FeatureServer layers expose a single geometryType (e.g. `esriGeometryPolygon`, `esriGeometryPoint`) defined at the layer level. A layer cannot contain mixed geometry types, as the geometry type is fixed by the service schema. While different sub-layers within the same FeatureServer may use different geometry types, each sub-layer will be treated as an independent layer in MapStore<br>
**Cesium rendering by geometry type**
`TiledBillboardCollection` will be used for point/multipoint layers under the `tile` strategy. For all other geometry types (polyline, polygon), these layers will automatically fall back to `bbox` strategy using `GeoJSONStyledFeatures` with a `camera.moveEnd` listener. This will be handled transparently by `getEffectiveStrategy`.<br>
**`drawingInfo.renderer` (future)**
Verified endpoints expose ESRI renderers in `drawingInfo`. Converting these to GeoStyler for default symbology would be a valuable future enhancement — not in scope for this implementation.<br>
**Point clustering (future)**
ArcGIS does not provide server-side clustering — `FeatureReductionCluster` in the ArcGIS JS SDK is entirely client-side. Rather than introducing the ArcGIS JS SDK as a dependency, clustering can be achieved natively using `ol/source/Cluster` in OpenLayers (wraps the existing `VectorSource` with a configurable pixel distance). For CesiumJS, a lightweight spatial indexing library such as `supercluster` could be evaluated. This would be a future enhancement applicable to all vector layer types, not specific to `arcgis-feature`.

